WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:07.545
NARRATOR: Begin by framing Course Purpose and
Faculty Role as a question about inputs,

2
00:00:07.545 --> 00:00:09.687
decisions, and observable results.

3
00:00:09.687 --> 00:00:16.843
NARRATOR: This is intended to allow faculty to
pilot potential new undergraduate offerings.

4
00:00:16.843 --> 00:00:23.536
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: A
professor proposes a new undergraduate course

5
00:00:23.536 --> 00:00:29.671
in theoretical computer science to test its
viability before full adoption.

6
00:00:29.671 --> 00:00:36.618
NARRATOR: On screen, display a slide with the
course title and a brief description of the

7
00:00:36.618 --> 00:00:37.921
faculty proposal process.

8
00:00:37.921 --> 00:00:44.116
NARRATOR: List the givens before choosing a
method, because an unstated assumption can

9
00:00:44.116 --> 00:00:45.546
change the conclusion.

10
00:00:45.546 --> 00:00:51.453
NARRATOR: This establishes the vocabulary and
scope needed for the remaining sections.

11
00:00:51.554 --> 00:00:58.037
NARRATOR: Now define Course Classification
Clusters precisely enough that another learner

12
00:00:58.037 --> 00:01:00.394
could test the definition.

13
00:01:00.394 --> 00:01:07.129
NARRATOR: Specific course instances will be
identified as belonging to the Theory cluster,

14
00:01:07.129 --> 00:01:10.238
the Security cluster, or both clusters.

15
00:01:10.238 --> 00:01:16.600
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: A
proposed course on cryptographic protocols is

16
00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:23.492
classified under the Security cluster, while a
course on computational complexity falls under

17
00:01:23.492 --> 00:01:25.082
the Theory cluster.

18
00:01:25.082 --> 00:01:31.928
NARRATOR: On screen, show a Venn diagram with
two overlapping circles labeled 'Theory

19
00:01:31.928 --> 00:01:38.773
Cluster' and 'Security Cluster', with a third
circle labeled 'Both Clusters' in the

20
00:01:38.773 --> 00:01:39.300
intersection.

21
00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:46.214
NARRATOR: Separate the formal definition from
an implementation detail, then identify what

22
00:01:46.214 --> 00:01:48.519
evidence would distinguish them.

23
00:01:48.519 --> 00:01:55.160
NARRATOR: A precise definition makes later
examples easier to evaluate and revise.

24
00:01:55.274 --> 00:02:01.136
NARRATOR: With the foundation in place,
examine how Faculty-Determined Details

25
00:02:01.136 --> 00:02:04.067
operates in a concrete sequence.

26
00:02:04.067 --> 00:02:09.256
NARRATOR: Specific course details (such as
prerequisites, course seminar, format,

27
00:02:09.256 --> 00:02:15.484
learning outcomes, assessment methods, and
resource needs) will be determined by the

28
00:02:15.484 --> 00:02:21.192
faculty member(s) who propose a specific
seminar course in this area.

29
00:02:21.192 --> 00:02:27.492
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: The
proposing faculty member sets the prerequisite

30
00:02:27.492 --> 00:02:34.317
to include discrete mathematics and defines
the format as a three-hour weekly lecture.

31
00:02:34.317 --> 00:02:41.443
NARRATOR: On screen, list the specific course
details (prerequisites, seminar, format,

32
00:02:41.443 --> 00:02:48.568
learning outcomes, assessment methods,
resource needs) with checkboxes for faculty to

33
00:02:48.568 --> 00:02:49.864
fill in.

34
00:02:49.864 --> 00:02:57.020
NARRATOR: Follow every arrow in order and name
the state before and after each transition.

35
00:02:57.020 --> 00:03:02.692
NARRATOR: The trace converts an abstract
statement into an inspectable sequence.

36
00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:09.383
NARRATOR: Next, compare the choices involved
in Stable Process Overview and make the

37
00:03:09.383 --> 00:03:10.900
decision criteria explicit.

38
00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:14.744
NARRATOR: This course examines current topics
in Theory.

39
00:03:14.744 --> 00:03:21.335
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: The
department follows a standardized workflow:

40
00:03:21.335 --> 00:03:28.525
faculty submit a proposal, assign cluster(s),
define details, and pilot the course.

41
00:03:28.525 --> 00:03:36.030
NARRATOR: On screen, create a linear flowchart
with four steps: Proposal Submission, Cluster

42
00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:39.494
Assignment, Detail Planning, and Course Pilot.

43
00:03:39.494 --> 00:03:46.019
NARRATOR: Compare alternatives against the
same constraints so the evaluation does not

44
00:03:46.019 --> 00:03:47.650
move the goalposts.

45
00:03:47.650 --> 00:03:54.541
NARRATOR: Using shared criteria turns a
preference into a defensible technical choice.

46
00:03:54.541 --> 00:04:01.600
NARRATOR: Name each dependency explicitly, and
keep labels and representations consistent

47
00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:03.525
throughout the analysis.

48
00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:10.600
NARRATOR: Apply Standards for Course Design to
a worked situation instead of treating it as

49
00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:11.990
an isolated definition.

50
00:04:11.990 --> 00:04:18.334
NARRATOR: Hash tables map keys to locations
and require a collision strategy.

51
00:04:18.334 --> 00:04:24.769
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: The
learning outcomes specify that students will

52
00:04:24.769 --> 00:04:30.668
analyze algorithmic correctness and resource
bounds, assessed through a final theoretical

53
00:04:30.668 --> 00:04:31.740
proof assignment.

54
00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:38.940
NARRATOR: On screen, display a table with
columns for 'Learning Outcome' and 'Assessment

55
00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:41.709
Method', filled with example entries.

56
00:04:41.709 --> 00:04:48.413
NARRATOR: Work from the concrete inputs to the
result, checking units, types, or invariants

57
00:04:48.413 --> 00:04:49.849
at each step.

58
00:04:49.849 --> 00:04:54.928
NARRATOR: The worked case shows both how to
apply the method and how to check it.

59
00:04:55.058 --> 00:05:01.188
NARRATOR: Examine the tradeoffs in Planning
Methods for Resources, including what improves

60
00:05:01.188 --> 00:05:03.742
and what becomes more difficult.

61
00:05:03.742 --> 00:05:10.492
NARRATOR: Asymptotic notation describes how
resource use grows with input size.

62
00:05:10.492 --> 00:05:16.934
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: The
faculty member lists required resources,

63
00:05:16.934 --> 00:05:23.375
including a whiteboard, projection system, and
specific textbooks on theoretical computer

64
00:05:23.375 --> 00:05:23.961
